 ],
 "literal": "If you ask who this man is, he is / with tiger-patterned armor, its beautiful designs shattered, / his mighty, beautiful chest pierced by shot arrows, / riding upon a male elephant like Yama, the god of death. / The elephant is like a ship sailing the ocean, / and he is like the moon in the midst of many stars, / as sword-bearing warriors, like a school of sharks, crowd around him. / Unconscious of his allies, his battle fury has risen. / Oh, may he return safely without any harm! / He is the lord of the land fenced by excellent waters, / where peacocks of the fertile fields shed feathers / which farmers gather along with sheaves of grain in the paddy fields, / and where fat fish and matured toddy abound.",
 "translation": "If you ask who this man is, he is the one riding a male elephant like Yama, the god of death, with his tiger-patterned armor shattered and his mighty, beautiful chest pierced by flying arrows. His elephant is like a ship sailing the ocean, and he is like the moon surrounded by many stars, as sword-bearing warriors crowd around him like a school of sharks. Unconscious of his own allies, his battle fury has risen. Oh, may he return safely without any harm! He is the lord of the land fenced by abundant waters, where peacocks of the fertile fields shed feathers that farmers gather along with sheaves of grain, and where fat fish and matured toddy abound.",
 "modern": "இவன் யார் என்று நீங்கள் கேட்டால், இவன் புலி போன்ற நிறமுடைய கவசம் சிதைந்துபோக, எய்யப்பட்ட அம்புகள் கிழித்த அழகிய மார்பினை உடையவன்; எமனைப் போல ஆண் யானையின் மேல் அமர்ந்திருப்பவன். கடலில் செல்லும் கப்பலைப் போல அவனது யானையும், பல விண்மீன்களுக்கு நடுவே இருக்கும் திங்களைப் போல அவனும் விளங்க, சுறா மீன் கூட்டம் போல வாளேந்திய வீரர்கள் அவனைச் சூழ்ந்து நிற்கிறார்கள். தன் நண்பர்களையும் அறியாதவாறு அவனது சினமும் வீரமும் பெருகியுள்ளது. அவன் எவ்விதத் தீங்கும் இன்றித் திரும்புவானாக! வயல்களில் மயில்கள் உதிர்த்த பீலிகளை உழவர்கள் நெற்கதிர்களோடு சேர்த்துத் தொகுக்கும் வளமும், கொழுத்த மீன்களும், கள்ளும் நிறைந்த, சிறந்த நீர்நிலைகளை எல்லையாகக் கொண்ட நாட்டின் தலைவன் இவன்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Uṟaiyūr Ēṇiccēri Muṭamōciyār is addressing onlookers or the enemy, praising the Chola king Muṭittalaik Kōperunaṟkiḷḷi during a fierce battle. The poet describes the king's terrifying appearance on the battlefield, riding an elephant like Yama, with his armor shattered and chest pierced by arrows. He is surrounded by his loyal warriors, likened to sharks surrounding a ship or stars surrounding the moon. The poet prays for his safe return. The poem transitions from the violent, chaotic imagery of the battlefield to the peaceful, fertile landscape of the Chola country, where peacocks shed feathers in fields and abundant fish and toddy abound. This contrast highlights the king's dual nature as a fierce protector in war and a benevolent ruler of a prosperous land.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}